[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Method Overloading vs Method Overriding in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polymorphism is a core concept in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) that allows developers to perform a single action in different ways. Two of the most common ways to achieve polymorphism in languages like Java, C++, and C# are Method Overloading and Method Overriding. Although their names sound similar, they serve entirely different purposes and operate under different rules."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Method Overloading?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method Overloading occurs when a class has multiple methods with the same name, but with different parameter lists (different number of parameters, different data types, or both). It is a compile-time (or static) polymorphism mechanism."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Happens within the same class."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Method signature must be different (parameters must vary)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Return type can be the same or different, but it alone cannot distinguish overloaded methods."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class MathOperations {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Method Overriding?"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method Overriding occurs when a subclass provides a specific implementation of a method that is already defined in its parent class. The version of the method executed is determined at runtime based on the object type, making it a runtime (or dynamic) polymorphism mechanism."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Involves two classes in an inheritance relationship (parent and child)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Method signature (name and parameters) must be exactly the same."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The return type must be the same or a covariant type."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences at a Glance"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Binding Time: Overloading is resolved at compile-time, while Overriding is resolved at runtime."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inheritance: Overloading does not require inheritance. Overriding strictly requires an inheritance relationship."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parameter Rules: Overloaded methods must have different parameters. Overridden methods must have the exact same parameters."},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding these distinctions allows software engineers to design clean, maintainable, and scalable class hierarchies by leveraging the appropriate type of polymorphism for their architecture."}]
